<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 https://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
  <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

  <parent>
    <groupId>com.thelastpickle.htap</groupId>
    <artifactId>htap-parent</artifactId>
    <version>0.1.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
  </parent>

  <artifactId>htap-common</artifactId>
  <name>Open HTAP stack: shared arithmetic</name>
  <description>
    The values the sink, the producer and the backend must spell identically.
    A sink and a backend disagreeing about event_shard produce queries that match
    nothing, which is why podman-compose.yml declares the bucket width and shard
    count once and both services read them.
  </description>

  <dependencies>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.junit.jupiter</groupId>
      <artifactId>junit-jupiter</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>
  </dependencies>

  <build>
    <plugins>
      <!--
        This module may declare no dependency outside test scope, ever, transitively
        included. Test scope holds junit-jupiter, which reaches neither image. The
        Python it replaces duplicated this arithmetic between the backend and the
        sink on purpose, so that the sink's image did not carry the backend's
        package and a change made for the dashboard could not silently change
        what the sink writes. A shared module keeps both properties only while it
        stays a leaf: the moment it wants Jackson, a driver or a config API, the
        sink is coupled to the backend's dependency tree again and the duplication
        was protecting something real.

        java.lang.Math, java.time and java.util.zip.CRC32 are enough. The
        enforcer is here rather than a comment alone because the failure is
        invisible: an added dependency compiles, passes and only shows up as an
        image that grew and a coupling nobody chose.
      -->
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
        <artifactId>maven-enforcer-plugin</artifactId>
        <executions>
          <execution>
            <id>no-dependency-outside-test-scope</id>
            <goals><goal>enforce</goal></goals>
            <configuration>
              <rules>
                <!--
                  Everything is banned and test scope is the one exception, rather than
                  compile, runtime and provided named as the three to ban: `system` is a
                  fourth that reaches the compile classpath, and a rule listing scopes
                  passes it. An allowlist cannot be short of a scope.
                -->
                <bannedDependencies>
                  <searchTransitive>true</searchTransitive>
                  <excludes>
                    <exclude>*</exclude>
                  </excludes>
                  <includes>
                    <include>*:*:*:*:test</include>
                  </includes>
                </bannedDependencies>
              </rules>
            </configuration>
          </execution>
        </executions>
      </plugin>
    </plugins>
  </build>
</project>
